<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Shared build and packaging properties live in src/Directory.Build.props. Only what differs
    between the two packages belongs here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Ank.DocToolkit.Extensions.DependencyInjection</PackageId>
    <!--
      NO NAMES AND NO COUNT IN HERE, deliberately, and it cost a stale nuget.org page to learn.
      This field said "six injectable interfaces" and then listed those six by name, while FIFTEEN
      shipped - so the package page advertised a smaller library than the one a consumer got, and
      nothing detected it. That is the same drift `check-readme-coverage.py` was written for, one
      field further out.

      A count here cannot be derived, so the fix is to stop making a claim that needs maintaining:
      describe the SHAPE (one interface per capability) rather than enumerate. The shape stays true
      as the surface grows. `scripts/check-package-description.py` fails the build if a count or a
      name is reintroduced here and drifts from the approved API.
    -->
    <Description>
      Dependency-injection registration for Ank.DocToolkit. services.AddDocToolkit() registers one
      injectable interface per capability - conversion between HTML, Markdown, DOCX, PDF, XLSX and
      PPTX, plus the DOCX/XLSX/PPTX/PDF editors - over the same pure-managed logic: no browser, no
      Chromium, no native binaries, no LibreOffice. For ASP.NET Core and worker-service consumers.
    </Description>
    <!-- Same three groups as the core package; see its csproj for why. The DI words stay first
         because someone searching for them is already most of the way to this package.

         SEVEN ADDED 2026-08-23 (F7). This list was WORSE than the core package's: it carried no
         conversion capability at all - not html-to-docx, not docx-to-pdf, not html-to-pdf - so the
         package could not be found by any capability it exposes, only by the fact that it does
         dependency injection. Someone searching `html to pdf dependency injection` had no way to
         reach it.

         Held to the capabilities plus the two constraints that matter most, rather than mirroring
         the core package's full list: this package's own differentiator IS the DI registration,
         and the core package is what a capability search should land on. See the core csproj for
         the dilution argument and for why this is unverified until F7 step 4 re-measures.

         FOUR MORE ADDED 2026-08-31 (F12, F13), held to the same short-list discipline rather than
         mirroring the core package's fifteen. `platform` and `binaries` are the F12 hyphen-fragment
         fix for the two constraint tags this package already carries (`cross-platform`,
         `no-native-binaries`) - the core csproj has the full measurement. `injection` and `protect`
         are added as explicit tags for concepts this package already implies in prose
         (`Dependency-injection registration`, and IDocxEditor/IWorkbookEditor/... mirroring
         Protect) - not chasing the core package's wider F13 list, since footnotes, mail-merge
         templates and conditional formatting are not this package's differentiator. -->
    <PackageTags>dependency-injection;aspnetcore;docx;pdf;xlsx;pptx;word;excel;openxml;html-to-docx;html-to-pdf;docx-to-pdf;browserless;no-native-binaries;linux;cross-platform;offline;csharp;dotnet;platform;binaries;injection;protect</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- README.md, THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt and the shared icon are packed by src/Directory.Build.props. -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Bumped to 0.46.0 for the mirror below: InspectSignatures/ValidateSignatures (all three
      editors, shipped 0.45.0) and ReadMetadata/WithMetadata (all three editors) plus
      WorkbookEditor.InspectFormulas/EvaluateFormulas (shipped 0.46.0). This package references the
      PUBLISHED core, never a ProjectReference, so it cannot wrap a method until that method has
      shipped - which is why the mirror lags one release, structurally, and why this bump is the
      first step rather than an afterthought.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Ank.DocToolkit" Version="[0.48.0, )" />

    <!--
      8.0.2, not 8.0.0, since 0.15.0. PDFsharp raised Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions
      from 6.0.0 to 8.0.3 in the core package's graph, and 8.0.3 requires
      DependencyInjection.Abstractions >= 8.0.2. A floor of [8.0.0, ) resolves to the LOWEST match -
      8.0.0 - which NuGet then reports as a package downgrade against that transitive requirement,
      and -warnaserror turns into a failed build.

      So this floor is not cosmetic: it is the minimum a consumer can actually resolve. Raising it
      raises theirs too, which is why it is called out in the changelog rather than left to be
      discovered at restore time.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="[8.0.2, )" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="[8.0.0, )" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Service implementations are internal sealed (never public - consumers depend on the
    interfaces only), but the test project instantiates them directly to exercise the
    delegation logic without going through the DI container. Grant that one assembly access.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DocToolkit.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- See src/DocToolkit/DocToolkit.csproj for why this is an attribute and not <IsTrimmable>. -->
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="IsTrimmable" Value="True" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
